{"level":30,"time":1778270843891,"pid":53120,"hostname":"Mac.lan","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1T4LmwPELQFjmMhr7XvBX","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided landscape plan (Sheet 28 of 35), here are the findings regarding trees and overhead electric lines:\n\n*   **Are overhead electric lines shown and marked?**\n    Yes. There is a line denoting an overhead electric utility running horizontally along the bottom (southern) edge of the site plan area, parallel to the property line/street. It is explicitly marked with the text \"**OHE**\" embedded within the line type itself (e.g., \"-----OHE-----\").\n\n*   **Are trees shown?**\n    Yes. Numerous trees are depicted throughout the plan using circular symbols indicating canopy spread. Many of these contain specific tags inside or next to them (e.g., \"M\", \"SY\", \"H.4\"). The legend in the bottom right corner defines some of these labels, such as:\n    *   \"**ST - STREET YARD TREE...**\"\n    *   \"**PL - PARKING LOT TREE...**\"\n    *   \"**M - MITIGATION TREE**\"\n\n*   **Do they appear in proximity to any proposed trees, and does it allow verification of clearances?**\n    Yes. The plan clearly shows the \"**OHE**\" line passing directly through, or immediately adjacent to, several of the circular tree canopy symbols located along the bottom edge of the property boundary. Because both the horizontal location of the overhead electric line and the proposed locations/canopy spreads of the trees are overlaid on the same 2D plan, it does allow a reviewer to verify horizontal clearances and identify potential conflicts between the utility lines and the trees."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb7435c0-6e4e-4832-a8a2-cd00cbe522f8","uuid":"339cbd70-2f91-4c0f-895c-68a177aed9cc","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided landscape plan (Sheet 28 of 35), here are the findings regarding trees and overhead electric lines:\n\n*   **Are overhead electric lines shown and marked?**\n    Yes. There is a line denoting an overhead electric utility running horizontally along the bottom (southern) edge of the site plan area, parallel to the property line/street. It is explicitly marked with the text \"**OHE**\" embedded within the line type itself (e.g., \"-----OHE-----\").\n\n*   **Are trees shown?**\n    Yes. Numerous trees are depicted throughout the plan using circular symbols indicating canopy spread. Many of these contain specific tags inside or next to them (e.g., \"M\", \"SY\", \"H.4\"). The legend in the bottom right corner defines some of these labels, such as:\n    *   \"**ST - STREET YARD TREE...**\"\n    *   \"**PL - PARKING LOT TREE...**\"\n    *   \"**M - MITIGATION TREE**\"\n\n*   **Do they appear in proximity to any proposed trees, and does it allow verification of clearances?**\n    Yes. The plan clearly shows the \"**OHE**\" line passing directly through, or immediately adjacent to, several of the circular tree canopy symbols located along the bottom edge of the property boundary. Because both the horizontal location of the overhead electric line and the proposed locations/canopy spreads of the trees are overlaid on the same 2D plan, it does allow a reviewer to verify horizontal clearances and identify potential conflicts between the utility lines and the trees."}]}
